with open('C:/projects/apache/school1/src/ptable/data/scratch_Am.json', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    data = json.load(f)

data['extract_html'] = '<p><b>Americium (Am)</b> is a fascinating synthetic, radioactive element with the atomic number 95. Part of the actinide series, it sits below europium on the periodic table and was named after the Americas to honor this connection. A silvery-white metal that tarnishes slowly in dry air, americium is unique among synthetic elements because it is widely found in everyday households! Its most famous application is in ionization smoke detectors, where a tiny amount of americium-241 is used to keep us safe from fires. While primarily synthesized in nuclear reactors, americium continues to play a vital role in industry, scientific research, and even deep-space exploration.</p>'

data['sections']['History'] = '<p>The story of americium begins during the intense era of the Manhattan Project. In the late autumn of 1944, a team of pioneering scientists—Glenn T. Seaborg, Leon O. Morgan, Ralph A. James, and Albert Ghiorso—first intentionally synthesized and identified americium at the University of California, Berkeley, and the University of Chicago\'s Metallurgical Laboratory.</p><p>They created it by bombarding plutonium with neutrons in a cyclotron. It was the fourth transuranium element (elements beyond uranium) to be discovered. Because it sits directly below europium in the newly restructured periodic table of the time, Seaborg suggested naming it "americium" after the Americas. The discovery was kept secret until after World War II, finally being announced to the public in November 1945 by Seaborg on a national radio show!</p>'

data['sections']['Occurrence'] = '<p>Americium is practically non-existent in nature. While minuscule trace amounts might exist in uranium ores as a result of rare natural nuclear reactions, essentially all americium on Earth today is synthetic. It is produced as a by-product in nuclear reactors when uranium and plutonium absorb neutrons. Over decades of atmospheric nuclear testing and reactor operations, small amounts of americium have been released into the environment, but commercial quantities are strictly synthesized in specialized facilities under heavy regulation.</p>'

data['sections']['Isotopes'] = '<p>All isotopes of americium are radioactive and none are stable. The most important and widely used isotope is <b>Americium-241</b>, which has a half-life of 432.2 years. It decays by emitting alpha particles and gamma rays, eventually turning into neptunium-237. Another significant isotope is <b>Americium-243</b>, which is the most stable isotope of the element, boasting a half-life of 7,370 years. Scientists use americium-243 for deeper chemical studies and to act as target material for synthesizing even heavier transuranic elements in particle accelerators.</p>'

data['sections']['Applications'] = '<p>Unlike many synthetic elements that exist only in laboratories, americium has tangible, everyday uses. Its most famous role is in <b>ionization smoke detectors</b>. A tiny fraction of a gram of americium-241 emits alpha particles that ionize the air inside the detector, creating a small electric current. When smoke enters the chamber, it disrupts this current, triggering the alarm.</p><p>In the industrial sector, americium-241 is used in thickness and density gauges to measure materials like glass, paper, and metal during manufacturing. When mixed with beryllium, it creates a portable neutron source used extensively for oil well logging, geological exploration, and highway construction.</p><p><b>Indian Context & Future Horizons:</b> In India, americium is utilized within the nation\'s nuclear research and industrial monitoring sectors. Indian scientific organizations rely on americium-beryllium neutron sources for soil moisture testing, agriculture, and geological surveys. Looking to the future, space agencies worldwide are researching americium-241 as a long-lasting power source (Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators, or RTGs) for deep-space probes. Because it generates heat through radioactive decay, it could power spacecraft exploring the outer planets where solar energy is insufficient.</p>'

